<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Arakkal kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1545 CE – 1819 CE (approx.) · capital Kannur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The only Muslim royal house of Kerala, the Ali Rajas of Cannanore, who ruled a coastal principality and the Lakshadweep islands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Arakkal family were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mappila">Mappila</a> Muslim ministers and admirals of the Kolathiri rulers of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kolathunadu">Kolathunadu</a>, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannur">Cannanore</a>. By the 16th century, as the Portuguese disrupted the coast, they became an autonomous power, holding the title Ali Raja, lord of the sea, and ruling the town and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshadweep">Lakshadweep</a> islands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Arakkal rulers followed matrilineal succession, so that the eldest member of the family, male or female, reigned; several women ruled with the title Arakkal Beevi. Their wealth came from trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, coir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowries</a> and from the islands. They opposed the Portuguese and later allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannur">Cannanore</a> in 1790, and the kingdom is conventionally dated to end in 1819. The family kept its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshadweep">Laccadive</a> islands until the British sequestrated them in 1875; they were formally surrendered for a pension in 1908. The Arakkal family survives in Kannur and maintains a museum in its old palace.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/arakkal-kingdom.jpg" alt="Arakkal kingdom" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Ziyad Arakkal Adiraja · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arakkal_flag_1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1545 CE – 1819 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kannur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Junumabe Ali Raja Beevi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malayalam</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arakkal Sultanate, Ali Rajas of Cannanore</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=arakkal-kingdom" aria-label="Arakkal kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6752 1736 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Arakkal kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7072 1992L7095 2035M7072 1992L7107 1971M7072 1992L7364 1601"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7095 2035h0M7107 1971h0M7364 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7072 1992h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7072 1992h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=arakkal-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
